🔷 1. What is RDS?
✅ Definition:
Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service) is a service that helps you create and manage SQL-based databases like:

MySQL
PostgreSQL
SQL Server
MariaDB
Oracle
Amazon Aurora
RDS stores data in tables (rows & columns) — like an Excel sheet.

🛠️ Use Cases:
Banking systems
E-commerce websites
Login systems
Billing and invoices
Applications with relationships between data (like Users → Orders)

🎯 Benefits:
Uses SQL (structured queries)
Data is structured and organized
Easy to use if you know MySQL or PostgreSQL
RDS handles backups, updates, and scaling

🔶 2. What is DynamoDB?
✅ Definition:
Amazon DynamoDB is a NoSQL database service. It stores data in a flexible, key-value format, like a big dictionary.
There are no tables with fixed columns. You just store whatever data you want per item (row).

🛠️ Use Cases:
Shopping cart sessions
IoT data (like temperature sensors)
Gaming leaderboards
Real-time chat apps
High-speed mobile apps

🎯 Benefits:
Very fast performance (even with millions of records)
Automatically scales up and down
No need to define tables or structure
Fully managed — no servers or patching

🔄 RDS vs DynamoDB – Simple Comparison Table
Feature	RDS (Relational)	DynamoDB (NoSQL)
Data Structure	Tables with rows & columns	Key-value pairs / JSON documents
Query Language	SQL	No SQL (uses AWS SDK or CLI)
Schema	Fixed (pre-defined columns)	Flexible (any format)
Use Cases	Banking, ERP, E-commerce apps	IoT, chat apps, real-time games
Speed	Slower for large scale	Super fast (millisecond latency)
Scaling	Manual or auto with Aurora	Automatic scaling
Learning Curve	Easier if you know SQL	Easier for developers/coders
Relationships	Supports joins and relations	No joins or relations
Backup & Restore	Automated by AWS	Automated by AWS
Example Format	SELECT * FROM users;	{"user_id": "123", "name": "Balu"}

